The initial Contractor training onsite for the majority of the Owner's personnel shall be delivered prior to the start of commissioning of the Plant. The objective of this training shall be to equip the Plant staff to undertake commissioning activities safely and effectively, under the direction of the Contractor's commissioning team and allow  them  to  participate  in  the  production  of  Commissioning  Documentation  and  development  of  the Commissioning Recording System.
The total  Production,  Operations,  Maintenance  and  Engineering  staff  complement  to  be  trained  on-site  will comprise; managers, engineers/team leaders; operations technicians and maintenance technicians.
Further experience of the Plant shall be gained during commissioning, prior to the Plant staff taking over Plant operation. The objective of this training shall be to achieve further development of the Owner's personnel. The Contractor shall allow for the additional coaching and assessment of the Owner's staff during this period by additional training personnel, to be supplied by the Owner. These additional training resources will also be available to assist in the commissioning activities.
Each element of the on-site training programme shall concentrate on specific systems/ plant areas and shall include but not be limited to the following components:
- Task steps;
- Standard performance for each step;
- Tools and equipment;
- Safety requirements;
- Resources (e.g. manufacturer's manuals, drawings and plant documents).

The Plant design shall include suitable security systems such as security fences. Facilities for security personnel such as a guard house shall also be included.
There shall also be building access control systems and building intrusion detection. The buildings to be fitted with this equipment shall be determined during the detailed design phase.
The Plant is to be protected from both casual and malicious intrusion. The protective measures which the Contractor shall implement shall include:
- perimeter security fence;
- CCTV system to monitor the perimeter and provide operational overviews and details of the site;
- intruder detection to buildings;
- access control system to site and buildings;
- gate intercom system to allow visitor access; and
- mechanised site access gate.
These measures shall be fully integrated to allow an automatic response to events to aid operator decisions.
The main security gate shall be provided with large screen display with multi camera monitoring.

- Valves  shall  be  insulated  with  suitable  valve  boxes  that  can  be  removed/replaced  for/following maintenance or preferably insulated with valve blankets that are held in position with Velcro straps or similar;
- The hot piping, subject to the expansion during the operation shall be insulated according to the fixed / movable support philosophy and with enough overlap to maintain necessary expansion of pipe,
- Vapor barriers should be applied on the insulation to extend the lifetime of the cladding material
- The  cladding  should  be  capable  to  easy  remove  and  reinstalling  of  the  material  during/after maintenance or inspection
- Wire shall be avoided wherever possible as a fixing material for insulation as it tends to cut into the insulation. Bands shall be the preferred method;
- Insulation of bends and elbows using straight pipe insulation cut into sections as required to obtain a reasonable smooth exterior shall be avoided by the Contractor. Preformed insulation shall be used wherever possible;
- The Contractor shall ensure that a permanent small hole is provided in flange lagging to act as a leakage indicator;
- All outdoor insulation shall be sealed by the Contractor using suitable compounds;
- The  Contractor  shall  state  the  amount  of  overlap  on  any  cladding,  particularly  with  respect  to corrugated sheeting;
- Piping insulation which runs on, for example, HRSG platform floors shall be capable of withstanding treading on by operators and maintenance staff.

The Contractor shall provide complete details of maintenance and spare parts requirements, including the routine maintenance and the major inspection and overhaul programmes needed in order to achieve the required Plant availability and performance. The maintenance requirement documentation shall include, but not be limited to:
