Electrical equipment shall be designed to ensure continuity of operation of the main Plant under all working conditions. The Plant shall also be capable of meeting all transient, abnormal and load shedding scenarios.
All reasonable precautions shall be taken in the design of equipment and of works to ensure the safety of personnel  concerned  with  the  operation  and  maintenance  of  works.  Safety,  isolation,  locking  and interlocking facilities shall comply with international standards and practice.
Electrical equipment shall be constructed to withstand the specified maximum short circuit currents and duration  without  the  temperature  exceeding  the  value  permitted  for  the  related  insulation  class.  The equipment shall be considered as being operated at steady state maximum permitted current prior to the inception of short circuit current.
The final temperature attained as a result of the passage of short circuit current shall not cause permanent damage, deformation or deterioration sufficient to reduce the normal operating characteristics below those specified.
Insulation materials shall be suitably finished so as to prevent deterioration of their qualities under the specified working conditions.
Anti-condensation heaters shall be provided for switchgear and control panels in areas without redundant air conditioning. The heaters shall be permanently connected without thermostats and energised via a local isolator supplied from a local distribution board. Anti-condensation heaters for motors shall be interlocked with the motor supply.
Electrical  Panels  shall  be  designed  to  facilitate  thermography  inspections  of  panel  by  provision  of  IR windows/ suitable design. (For Gas turbine package, IR windows are not applicable.)
Auxiliary  power  systems  shall  be  arranged  to  minimise  operational  constraint  and  be  capable  of maintaining or rapidly restoring the full continuous power output in the event of a single component or circuit failure.
