The generator circuit breaker has to fulfil the requirements according to IEEE (ANSI) C37.013 and IEC 62271 series of standards;
Exclusively circuit breakers of the SF6 type with 125 VDC motor-driven stored energy mechanisms are to be provided. All CBs shall be fitted with operational counters;
All current and voltage transformers are to be provided in accordance with IEC 61869 series of standards;
Motor-driven earthing switches of short-circuit-proof design are to be provided. The necessary interlocks with the 380 kV line isolator, generator circuit breaker etc. are to be provided
The generator shall be connected to the generator circuit breaker, excitation transformer, generator stepup-transformer and unit auxiliary transformer via Isolated Phase Bus bars (IPB), which shall be enclosed in an insulated aluminium bus duct slightly pressurized with clean cooled dry air, including the necessary tee-offs.
The design and mechanical construction of all bus bars, boards and links shall safely withstand the thermal and dynamic stresses under normal operation and fault current conditions. Provisions shall be made for free  expansion  of  conductors  and  enclosures  due  to  thermal  stresses  by  means  of  expansion  pieces. Transmission  of  vibrations  occurring  at  the  generator  or  transformers  to  the  busbar  systems  shall  be avoided by use of flexible and adequately dimensioned connection tapes.
The system shall contain in addition to the main circuit busducts:
- a. Terminal boxes for the generator enclosing the main terminals and current transformers around the terminal bushings;
- b. Voltage Transformer, Surge Arrester and Protective Capacitors should be located inside GCB cubicle at the unit-transformer side.
- c. Generator neutral busducts and neutral earthing equipment cubicle;
- d. Removable three phase short circuit bridge for testing purposes, rated according to the rated current of the system and located between the generator and generator circuit breaker; and
- e. Tee-offs for the transformers (unit, unit auxiliary and excitation).
The internals of the line side busducts shall be arranged with a positive pressure to keep the system clean and dry. The busducts are to be naturally cooled.
The connections to the generator, generator circuit breaker and transformers shall have flexible joints to prevent the transfer of mechanical loads and forces. The enclosure connections to transformers shall be by means of expansion bellows made of electrically isolating and easily removable material for access to the transformer terminals.

The  step-up  transformer  on-line  condition  monitoring  system  shall  consist  of  sub-systems  for  specific monitoring tasks and shall comprise at least the following main functions:
- -Electrical  real-time  partial  discharge  (PD)  monitoring  system  in  accordance  to  IEC  60270  for permanent installation on-site;
- -Gas-in-oil  detection  (DGA)  by  monitoring  dissolved  hydrocarbon  and  combustible  gases  in  oil, especially the significant gases for partial discharge activities;
- -On-load tap changer (OLTC) monitoring system;
- -Temperature supervision system.
The  transformer  oil  must  be  non-corrosive,  non-aging,  hydro-treated,  pure  virgin  of  naphthane  base petroleum  product,  without  any  anti  oxidant  (uninhibited)  ,  free  from  additives,  extremely  good  heat transfer  characteristic  and  shall  have  properties  such  as  to  avoid  formation  of  copper  sulphide  under continuous heavy loading conditions in accordance with latest edition of IEC 60296. The liquid shall not contain,  nor  be  contaminated  with,  polychlorinated  biphenyls  (PCBs)  and  shall  be  of  low  sulphur corrosiveness and shall be in accordance with latest edition of IEC 60296: 2003. The transformer oil shall pass the corrosion tests according to ASTM D1275 method B, IEC 62535 and DIN 51353.
Each  Unit  Step  up  main  transformer  shall  also  be  equipped  with  on  line  partial  discharge  monitoring system,  whose  functioning  is  based  on  the  simultaneous  use  of  three  unconventional  methods  of  PD detection: high-frequency (HF), ultra-high frequency (UHF), and acoustic emission (AE). PD system shall be integrated according to the IEC 61850 standard with SCADA (Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition) system registering voltage, active power and oil temperature of the monitored unit.
Generator step-up transformer impedance voltage to be minimum 15%, however shall be adjusted based on the fault level contribution limitation.
Bushings, surge arresters and cable sealing ends utilized for outdoor connections shall have a minimum creepage distance as per IEC 61643 for low voltage, and IEC 60815 & IEC 60601-1 ("Guide for selection of insulators with respect to polluted conditions") for high voltage equipment. Self-cleaning polymer bushings will be accepted with creepage distance of 42 mm/kV.

