Segregation  of  the  various  services  and  voltage  levels  shall  be  achieved  by  use  of  separate  trays  and marshalling boxes for each voltage grade of cable used. The highest voltage cables shall be laid on the bottom rack with lower voltage cables next. Control and protection cables shall be laid on the highest levels. For the data highway communication link, spatially separated routing with the cables running in galvanised steel conduits of approved type shall be supplied and installed. If  cables  of  separate units are included in the same cable route, they shall be segregated by the use of barriers. Cables shall be suitably derated as appropriate to the requirements of the installation. Unnecessary cable joints shall be avoided. Cables shall be permanently identified with steel or plastic cable markers. Cables laid direct in ground shall be armoured. Multicore cable cores shall be terminated using crimps or other suitable termination. Bare cores will not be accepted. Cores shall be provided with some permanent means of indelible identification. This may include colour coding or identification ferrules. Fire  barriers  shall  be  installed  on  all  cables  at  penetrations  through  building  walls  and  other  building structures. The medium and low voltage cables shall be plastic-insulated copper cables. For cables that are exposed to ambient temperatures above 60°C Teflon or silicone cables are to be provided. The cables must be suitable for laying indoors, in the open (direct or indirect sunlight), in ducts, on trays, in the ground and in water.
